\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{A Reattributed Panel Painting from the Workshop
of the Master of the Brunswick Diptych}
\author{H. Vermeulen}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{introduction}
A small oak panel in the storerooms of the municipal museum
has long been catalogued as an anonymous fifteenth-century
copy after a lost original.
Recent dendrochronological dating of the panel support,
together with the pigment study summarised in
Section~\ref{sec:tech}, now permits a firmer attribution.
This article presents the documentary and technical evidence
and proposes a place for the panel within the workshop's
output.

\input{chapters/analysis}

\section{Provenance}
\label{chap:prov}
The panel first appears in the inventory of a guild chapel
in 1623, where it is described as an image of Saint Anne.
As noted in Section~\ref{introduction}, the municipal museum
acquired the piece with the bequest of 1891, and the early
inventory entries are reprinted in the appendix.
Nothing in the seventeenth-century records contradicts a
continuous local history.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:concl}
The combined evidence of the documentary record in
Section~\ref{chap:prov} and the technical findings argues
for an origin within the workshop rather than the master's
own hand.
A fuller catalogue of the workshop's panels is in
preparation.

\end{document}

